## Event Sourcing Pattern

## Traditional State Storage vs Event Sourcing

```
Traditional (current state):
  orders table: { id: 1, status: 'shipped', total: 99.99 }
  → You know current state. You lost all history.

Event Sourcing (append-only log):
  events table:
    { id: 1, type: 'OrderPlaced',   data: { total: 99.99 }, at: T1 }
    { id: 2, type: 'PaymentReceived', data: { amount: 99.99 }, at: T2 }
    { id: 3, type: 'OrderShipped',   data: { trackingId: 'XYZ' }, at: T3 }
  → Replay events → current state. Full history preserved.
```

## Event Store Implementation

```typescript
interface DomainEvent {
  id: string;
  aggregateId: string;
  type: string;
  data: unknown;
  occurredAt: Date;
  version: number;
}

class EventStore {
  async append(event: DomainEvent): Promise<void> {
    await db.insert('events', event); // append only, never update
  }

  async getEvents(aggregateId: string): Promise<DomainEvent[]> {
    return db.query(
      'SELECT * FROM events WHERE aggregate_id = $1 ORDER BY version',
      [aggregateId]
    );
  }
}

// Reconstruct Order state by replaying events
async function loadOrder(orderId: string): Promise<Order> {
  const events = await eventStore.getEvents(orderId);
  return events.reduce(applyEvent, new Order());
}
```

## Benefits

```
✅ Complete audit trail — every change is recorded
✅ Temporal queries — reconstruct state at any point in time
✅ Event replay — rebuild projections, fix bugs retroactively
✅ Natural fit with CQRS — events populate read models
✅ Decoupled consumers — multiple services subscribe to the same events
```

## Challenges

```
❌ Eventual consistency — read models lag slightly behind
❌ Event versioning — schema changes require migration strategy
❌ Large event histories — snapshots needed to avoid slow replays
❌ Complexity — steep learning curve for the team
```

## Snapshot Optimisation

```
Problem: 10,000 events → slow replay
Solution: store a snapshot every N events
  Load from snapshot → replay only events after snapshot version
```

## CQRS Pattern

## Origin

Bertrand Meyer's CQS: a method either **changes state** (command) or **returns a value** (query) — never both.

Greg Young extended this to the architecture level: separate the write and read **models**.

## Architecture

```
                    ┌──────────────┐
  Client ──────────▶│  Commands    │
  (mutations)       │  (Write Side)│
                    │              │
                    │  Aggregates  │
                    │  Domain Logic│
                    │  Repository  │
                    └──────┬───────┘
                           │ emits events
                    ┌──────▼───────┐
                    │  Event Bus   │
                    └──────┬───────┘
                           │
                    ┌──────▼───────┐
                    │  Projections │ ← event handlers update read models
                    │  (Read Side) │
                    │              │
                    │  Read DB     │ ← optimised for reads (Redis, PG views)
                    └──────┬───────┘
                           │
  Client ◀────────────────┘
  (queries)
```

## Implementation

```typescript
// COMMAND: mutation
interface PlaceOrderCommand {
  customerId: string;
  items: { productId: string; qty: number }[];
}

async function placeOrder(cmd: PlaceOrderCommand): Promise<void> {
  const order = Order.create(cmd.customerId, cmd.items);
  await orderRepo.save(order);
  await events.publish(new OrderPlaced(order));
  // Returns void — commands don't return data
}

// QUERY: read model
interface OrderSummaryQuery { customerId: string; }

async function getOrderSummaries(q: OrderSummaryQuery): Promise<OrderSummary[]> {
  // Read from denormalised projection — simple, fast
  return redis.get(`orders:${q.customerId}`);
}

// PROJECTION: event handler updates the read model
async function onOrderPlaced(event: OrderPlaced): Promise<void> {
  const summary = buildSummary(event);
  await redis.set(`orders:${event.customerId}`, summary);
}
```

## Testing Types

## Test Pyramid

```
           /\
          /E2E\ ← fewest, slowest, most fragile
         /──────\
        /Integr.  \ ← medium, slower, catch wiring
       /────────────\
      /  Unit Tests   \ ← most, fastest, catch logic
     /──────────────────\
```

## Unit Test

```typescript
// Tests one function/class in isolation
// All dependencies are mocked
describe('calculateDiscount', () => {
  it('applies 20% discount for premium users', () => {
    expect(calculateDiscount(100, { isPremium: true })).toBe(80);
  });
});

Speed: milliseconds | Scope: one function | Mocks: yes
```

## Integration Test

```typescript
// Tests components working together — real DB, real service
describe('UserRepository', () => {
  it('saves and retrieves a user', async () => {
    const repo = new SupabaseUserRepository(testDb);
    await repo.save(testUser);
    const found = await repo.findById(testUser.id);
    expect(found).toEqual(testUser);
  });
});

Speed: seconds | Scope: multiple components | Mocks: external only
```

## Smoke Test

```
Run after each deployment:
  1. GET / → 200
  2. POST /api/auth/login → 200
  3. GET /api/products → 200

Fast subset (~2 min) that verifies core paths work.
If smoke tests fail → rollback immediately, don't run full suite.
```

## Regression Test

```typescript
// Documents a bug that was fixed and prevents it from returning
describe('Order total calculation (regression: #JIRA-123)', () => {
  it('does not apply discount twice for bundled products', () => {
    const order = new Order([bundle1, bundle2]);
    expect(order.total()).toBe(90); // was incorrectly 81 before fix
  });
});
```

## Summary

```
Unit        → Is the logic correct in isolation?
Integration → Do components work together?
Smoke       → Is the deployment basically alive?
Regression  → Does old behaviour still work after new changes?
```

## Offloading Work from the Database

## 1. Caching (Most Impactful)

```typescript
// Cache-aside pattern
async function getProduct(id: string): Promise<Product> {
  const cached = await redis.get(`product:${id}`);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  const product = await db.query('SELECT * FROM products WHERE id = $1', [id]);
  await redis.setEx(`product:${id}`, 3600, JSON.stringify(product)); // TTL 1h
  return product;
}
// Reduces DB reads by ~90% for hot products
```

## 2. Read Replicas

```typescript
// Route reads to replica, writes to primary
const primary = createPool({ host: 'db-primary' });
const replica = createPool({ host: 'db-replica' });

async function getOrders(userId: string) {
  return replica.query('SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = $1', [userId]);
}
async function createOrder(order: Order) {
  return primary.query('INSERT INTO orders ...', [...]);
}
```

## 3. Connection Pooling (PgBouncer)

```
Without PgBouncer:
  1000 concurrent users → 1000 DB connections → DB overwhelmed

With PgBouncer:
  1000 users → PgBouncer → 20 actual DB connections (pooled)
```

## 4. Materialised Views

```sql
-- Pre-compute expensive dashboard aggregation
CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW daily_sales AS
  SELECT date_trunc('day', created_at) as day,
         SUM(total) as revenue,
         COUNT(*) as orders
  FROM orders
  GROUP BY 1;

-- Refresh nightly (no query-time aggregation)
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY daily_sales;
```

## 5. Message Queue for Write Offloading

```typescript
// Analytics events: don't write to DB on every page view
queue.publish('page_view', { url, userId, timestamp });

// Consumer batches 1000 events and writes once
queue.consume(async (events) => {
  await db.batchInsert('page_views', events); // one INSERT, 1000 rows
});
```

## 6. Elasticsearch for Search

```
Full-text search in PostgreSQL → expensive, slow at scale
Elasticsearch → purpose-built for search, handles millions of docs

Sync strategy: write to PG (authoritative) → event → sync to ES
```
